PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
- Full consensus has been reached across all participants for Choice F ("None of the above").
- Colleague 2 retracted their earlier vote for E (Countour) and now concurs that lines 435–436 ("Argus, the noble countour, / Sete to rekene in hys countour") constitute an intentional *rime riche* (identical/equivocal rhyme: agent noun "reckoner" rhymed with concrete noun "abacus/counting board"), a standard and sophisticated Middle English poetic convention.
- All other candidates (Wente, Here, Fool, Hool) occur in standard octosyllabic rhyming couplets throughout *The Book of the Duchess*.
- Since Chaucer indeed rhymes every word listed in options A through E, Choice F remains the definitively correct answer.
}

